Polly spend for the three phase chapters — measured at the meter, 2026-09-18
===========================================================================

WHY THIS RAN
  The same reason as 2026-09-16 (session-logs/polly-spend-20260916-video.log):
  the only authority on what was billed is the meter. `video/out/audio/` cannot
  bound it — `render.py --verify` does `shutil.rmtree(OUT / "audio")` between
  its two renders on purpose, so the surviving cache is one pass wide however
  many were paid for (feedback_meter_not_artifact).

MEASURED — CloudWatch AWS/Polly RequestCharacters, Operation=SynthesizeSpeech
  window 2026-09-17T00:00 .. 2026-09-19T00:00, Period=3600, Sum + SampleCount
  (timestamps as returned, +08:00)

  2026-09-18T12:00    17263 characters    74 requests
  2026-09-18T13:00    31560 characters   134 requests
  2026-09-18T14:00     8040 characters    38 requests
  TOTAL               56863 characters   246 requests

DECOMPOSITION — exact, to the request and to the character
  A pass is NOT 62 requests. There are 31 scenes across the four scripts
  (overview 10, before/during/after 7 each) and two languages, so 62 texts —
  but the three chapters end on the SAME `verify` scene, word for word, in both
  languages. `synthesize()` keys its cache on the REQUEST (voice | engine |
  language | text), so those four texts are one request each, not four:

    62 scene-language texts -> 58 distinct -> 58 requests / 13436 characters
      duplicates: {before,during}/verify en (358 ch) and zh (140 ch),
                  both served from after/verify
      per pass: 9860 generative (en, Ruth) + 3576 neural (zh, Zhiyu)

  Against that unit the meter resolves with nothing left over:

    4 x verify pass   4 x 58 = 232 req   4 x 13436 = 53744 ch
    1 x `--video before`     14 req                  3119 ch
    ------------------------------------------------------------
                            246 req                 56863 ch   <- the meter

  The four passes are two `--verify` runs (each renders twice with fresh
  synthesis): the first after the phase-chapter scripts landed, the second
  after the label-clearance fix to `scenes.py` invalidated it. The 14-request
  entry is the single-video render used to read one frame back before paying
  for a second verification — 2278 en + 841 zh characters, which is `before`
  exactly, and it billed rather than hit the cache.

PRICE — generative $30/M, neural $16/M (rates confirmed against Cost Explorer
        in the 2026-09-16 log; unchanged)
    4 passes         39440 generative  $1.1832    14304 neural  $0.228864
    before, once      2278 generative  $0.06834     841 neural  $0.013456
    ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    TOTAL 2026-09-18                              $1.4939

  No interval this time. The 2026-09-16 figure was a range ($0.72–$0.77)
  because 3508 characters belonged to a superseded draft whose engine split was
  not derivable; here every character is attributed to a known voice, so the
  number is a point.

  One cold `--verify` of all four videos, going forward: 2 x $0.3530 = $0.706.

CUMULATIVE for the /design explainer, both rounds
    2026-09-15/16   28476 ch   120 req   $0.72 .. $0.77
    2026-09-18      56863 ch   246 req   $1.4939
    ---------------------------------------------------
                    85339 ch   366 req   $2.21 .. $2.26

  Against the $95 repo ceiling. No other AWS spend in this round: no new
  resources, and the publish is S3 PUT + CloudFront transfer of 26.4 MB.

WHY THE COST IS DOMINATED BY RE-VERIFICATION, ON THE RECORD
  Of the $1.49, $1.41 is verification and $0.08 is rendering something new.
  That is the honest shape of the rule this pipeline holds — a render is only
  publishable if two independent renders with fresh synthesis agree byte for
  byte, so every edit to a scene template costs a full re-synthesis of all four
  videos even when no narration changed. The audio is identical across the two
  verifies (the eight durations match to 0.1 s), and the cache still cannot be
  reused, because reusing it would mean the second pass proved nothing about
  synthesis. The alternative — hash-verify frames and trust the audio — was not
  taken: `feedback_smoke_test_narrower_than_production` is in this repo's
  history precisely because a narrower check authorised a wider claim.
